expanded_sketch:
The introduction only gives a high-level roadmap for proving Theorem~\ref{thm:main}: next the authors set up the regularity structure framework and divide the strategy into a deterministic and probabilistic statement, then prove the former. \begin{lemma}\label{lem:equivalence}
Under Assumptions \ref{asn:data} and \ref{asn:area-model}, if $U\in\cD_{\sol}(\Gamma)$ is a solution to \eqref{eq:abstract-gPAM-old}
with respect to the model $(\Pi,\Gamma)$ defined in \eqref{eq:area-model},
then $V=\iota U$ belongs to $\cD_{\sol}'(\Gamma')$ and is a solution to
\begin{equ}\label{eq:abstract-gPAM-new}
V=\mathcal{P}\Big(\widehat{gg'}(V)\,\<AEta>\Big)
\end{equ}
with respect to the model $(\Pi',\Gamma')$.
\end{lemma}
They then formulate precisely the latter. \begin{theorem}\label{thm:convergence}
Let $\kappa\in(0,1/100)$ and let $c_\rho>0$ be the constant defined in \eqref{eq:c-whitenoise}. Let $\<Eta>$ be $c_\rho$ times a $2$-dimensional spatial white noise and let $\<EtaIEta>$ be the Wick product of $\<Eta>$ and $\<IEta>$. Then they almost surely satisfy Assumption \ref{asn:area-model} and $(\hat \Pi^\eps,\hat \Gamma^\eps)\to(\Pi,\Gamma)$ in law in $\cM_\kappa$, where $(\Pi, \Gamma)$ is as in \eqref{eq:area-model}.
\end{theorem}
Finally, in establishing the main theorem, they give the proof of the main result modulo these two statements. The probabilistic part (the preceding theorem) is then proved later as a statement on the convergence of a sequence of BPHZ models, with the note that the limit is \emph{not} a BPHZ model.

expanded_theorem:
\label{thm:main}
Let
\begin{assumption}\label{asn:data}
One has $g\in C^5_b$ and $\psi\in C^\theta(\T^2)$ for some $\theta\in(0,1/4)$.
\end{assumption}
hold.
For any $\eps>0$ let $u_\eps$ be the solution to
\begin{equ}\label{eq:gPAM_regular}
	\partial_t u_\eps = \Delta u_\eps + g(u_\eps) \eps^{\frac12} \partial_{x_1} \xi_\eps-C_\eps g'g(u_\eps)-\bar C_\eps (g')^3g(u_\eps)-\hat C_\eps g''g'g^2(u_\eps)
\end{equ}
on $\T^2$ with initial condition $\psi$.
There exists a sequence of constants $C_\eps,\bar C_\eps, \hat C_\eps$ and a constant $c_\rho>0$ such that $u_\eps$  converges in law as $\eps\to 0$ in $C([0,1]\times\T^2)$ to the renormalized solution $u$ of the SPDE
	\begin{equ}\label{eq:gPAM_limit}
		\partial_t u = \Delta u + c_\rho g'g(u) \eta
	\end{equ}
	on $\T^2$ with initial condition $\psi$, 	where $\eta$ is a  spatial white noise.,

