expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, it suffices to explain the argument for $D_n$ (the proofs for $R_n$ and $D_n$ are “essentially identical”). Define the “logarithmic correction”
\[
\psi(n):=\frac{E(D_n)}{n}.
\]
The objective is to estimate “the difference between $\psi(n)$ and $\psi(2n)$ as precisely as possible,” and the main contribution is to establish that there exists $\epsilon>0$ such that for all $n\ge 1$,
\begin{equation}\label{GOAL1}
\psi (2n) = \psi (n) \Big\{ 1 - \frac{\log 2 }{2 \log n} + O \big( (\log n)^{-1 - \epsilon} \big) \Big\},
\end{equation}
Then the main theorem can be deduced by combining the displayed equation above with the fact that “$\psi$ is a slowly varying function”; we use the following lemma.

\begin{lemma}\label{deriv}
Suppose that a function $\psi : \mathbb{N} \to (0, \infty)$ satisfies the following two conditions: 

\begin{itemize}
\item[(I)] there exists $c >0$ such that for all $n \ge 1$ and $k \in [n, 2n]$ 
$$\psi (k) = \psi (n) \big\{ 1 + O \big( (\log n)^{-c} \big) \big\}.$$

\item[(II)] there exists $\epsilon > 0$ such that for all $n \ge 1$ 
$$ \psi (2n) = \psi (n) \Big\{ 1 - \frac{\log 2 }{2 \log n} + O \big( (\log n)^{-1 - \epsilon} \big) \Big\}.$$ 

\end{itemize}
Set $\delta = \min \{ c, \epsilon \}$. Then there exists $a > 0$ such that 
$$\psi (n) = a  (\log n)^{-\frac{1}{2}} \Big\{ 1 + O \Big( \big( \log n \big)^{-\delta} \Big) \Big\},$$

\end{lemma}

For the displayed equation above, let $D_n'$ be the graph distance between $S(n)$ and $S(2n)$ in ${\cal G}_{n,2n}$, and use the identity
\[
\psi(n)-\psi(2n)=\frac{E(D_n+D_n'-D_{2n})}{2n},
\]
so one must analyze $E(D_n+D_n'-D_{2n})$. The key idea is to exploit “shortcuts” coming from intersections: if there exist $0\le k\le n\le l\le 2n$ with $S(k)=S(l)$, then “as $l-k$ becomes larger, the graph ${\cal G}_{0,2n}$ admits a more substantial shortcut,” so $D_n+D_n'-D_{2n}$ becomes smaller.

Introduce the maximal intersection length
\[
M=\max\{l-k:0\le k\le n\le l\le 2n,\ S(k)=S(l)\},
\]
and estimate $E(D_n+D_n'-D_{2n})$ by “considering cases according to the value of $M$” (in practice via a related quantity $L$). “Roughly speaking, conditioned on $M=m$, the quantity $D_n+D_n'-D_{2n}$ can, with high conditional probability, be approximated by $m\psi(m)$,” so
\begin{equation}\label{sumM}
\sum_{m} m \psi (m) P (M=m).
\end{equation}
By obtaining “a sharp estimate of the distribution of $M$,” one derives “precise upper and lower bounds” on the equation above, yielding the displayed equation establishing the key asymptotic relation for $\psi(2n)$ versus $\psi(n)$. We now record the two bounds used for this step:

\begin{proposition}\label{upper}
We have 
$$\psi(n/2) - \psi (n) \le  \frac{\log 2}{2 \log n}  \psi (n/2)  \big\{ 1 + O \big( (\log n)^{-1/100} \big) \big\}.$$
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}\label{lower}
We have 
$$\psi(n/2) - \psi (n) \ge  \frac{\log 2}{2 \log n}  \psi (n/2)  \big\{ 1 + O \big( (\log n)^{-c} \big) \big\}.$$
\end{proposition}

A key difficulty is controlling “all error terms” to match the order in the displayed equation establishing the key asymptotic relation for $\psi(2n)$ versus $\psi(n)$; in particular, estimating the distribution of $M$ “with sufficient precision,” which is reduced to “long-range intersections of the random walk” and handled using the following proposition.

\begin{proposition}\label{fnk}
The following equation holds for all $n, k \ge 2$.
\begin{align*}
f (n;k) = \frac{1}{2 \log n} \, \log \Big[ 1 + \frac{1}{k^{2} +2k } \Big] \,  \bigg\{ 1 + O \bigg( \frac{1}{k(\log n)^{\frac{3}{25}}} \bigg) \bigg\},
\end{align*}
where the constants used in $O$ are universal and do not depend on $n$ and $k$. 
\end{proposition}

