\item Next they recall variational and spectral ingredients; they state ``the spectral properties of the linearized operator $Z_{\theta,\lambda}$'' and ``coercivity of the energy near a two-bubble,'' which are ``very important to estimate $g$''; they ``use the energy conservation to deal with this.''
\item Then they estimate modulation parameters by imposing orthogonality conditions so that ``the terms linear in $g$ in the modulation equations disappeared'' (with ``essentially a unique choice"). Under bootstrap assumptions, they use the following lemma.

\begin{lemma}
		\label{lem:mod}
		Let $c > 0$ be an arbitrarily small constant. Let $T_0 < 0$ with $|T_0|$ large enough (depending on $c$)
		and $T < T_1 \leq T_0$. Suppose that for $T \leq t \leq T_1$ there holds
